ocations, 'by_name': by_name, 'total': total}\\n\"}\n\nBut note: I need to use actual newlines in the JSON string? No, JSON strings must use \\n for newlines. In the above I used actual newlines, but that's not valid JSON. The JSON specification requires that a string is a sequence of characters escaped. So I must replace newlines with \\n. In my earlier manual escaping, I used \\\\n but that's actually a backslash followed by n. The correct escape for newline in JSON is \\n (a single backslash-n). In the string representation, when I write the JSON, I need to include the backslash character itself followed by n. So in the JSON text, it should appear as \"line1\\nline2\". So I need to produce a string where newlines are replaced by the two characters backslash and n. In my manual construction, I wrote \\\\n to represent a backslash and n in the JSON text? Actually in a string literal in many contexts, \\\\n means a literal backslash followed by n. But in JSON, to include a newline, you write \\n. So when writing the JSON payload, I need to actually put a backslash character and then the letter n. So I need to escape it appropriately in my response.\n\nSince this is a text response, I will produce the JSON with actual escape sequences: i.e., for each newline in the source, I'll write \\n. For each double quote inside the source, I'll write \\\". So the final JSON string will contain these escape sequences as literal characters.\n\nI think I'll now produce the final response as a single JSON object. I'll ensure it's valid.\n\nLet me write the source string with escapes:\n\nI'll use a text editor mentally:\n\nLine by line:\n\ndef solve(case):\\n    segments = case['segments']\\n    budget = case['budget']\\n    n = len(segments)\\n    if not isinstance(budget, int) or budget < 0:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Budget must be non-negative integer\\\")\\n    for seg in segments:\\n        for key in ('name', 'importance', 'minimum', 'capacity'):\\n            if key not 